CAT = "mtguardIncidents"; SEG = "INC"
# Jobsplus Award, Module 2 outcomes: deal with a bomb threat together with co-workers and superiors,
# carry out initial security investigations or inquiries, produce reports that comply with relevant
# legislation; S.L. 389.01 First Schedule (observation, investigation, inquiry, action, controls).
Q = [
("During a bomb threat call, which detail should a guard try to note?",
 "Background sounds on the line", ["The caller's credit rating", "His own shift pattern", "The weather outside"],
 "Traffic, music, machinery or voices in the background can help the police locate the caller, alongside his exact words, accent and the time of the call."),
("After a bomb threat, why should a guard write down the caller's words straight away?",
 "Memory fades fast", ["To post them online", "To show the client later", "Only if the call was long"],
 "Exact words written at once are more accurate. The police use them to judge how serious the threat is."),
("Why should a guard not hang up the phone line used for a bomb threat?",
 "It may help trace the call", ["It saves on phone costs", "It stops other calls", "It is a basic rule of phone courtesy"],
 "On some systems an open line or the call record helps the authorities identify where the call came from."),
("A rucksack has been left for an hour under a bench at a Valletta bus terminus kiosk the guard protects. Staff do not know whose it is. What is his first step?",
 "Ask nearby people if it is theirs", ["Open it to find a name", "Kick it to test its weight", "Carry it to the lost property"],
 "Most unattended bags are simply lost. Asking around first is quick; if no owner is found and anything about it is suspicious, the guard keeps people back, never touches it and calls 112."),
("What should a guard note about a suspicious item for the police?",
 "Where it is, what it looks like", ["Its likely value", "Its brand name", "Who owns the building"],
 "The location, size, description and when it was found help the police decide how to deal with it."),
("Who decides whether a building is evacuated after a bomb threat?",
 "Management, on police advice", ["The caller who rang", "Any visitor present", "The newest guard on duty at the time"],
 "The site's plan names who decides, usually senior management advised by the police. Guards help carry it out."),
("Which question belongs in an initial investigation by a guard?",
 "What happened, when and where", ["Who is to blame and why", "What punishment fits", "How much to fine him"],
 "An initial inquiry gathers facts and witnesses and preserves evidence. Blame and punishment are for the police and courts."),
("After a break-in, why should a guard keep people out of the room?",
 "To preserve evidence", ["To keep it warm", "To tidy it up later", "To save on cleaning"],
 "Footprints, fingerprints and moved objects can identify the offender. Every extra person risks destroying them."),
("Who should a guard let into a protected crime scene?",
 "Only police and emergency crews", ["Curious staff members", "The press photographers", "Any manager who asks to look around"],
 "Access is limited to those who need to be there, mainly the police and emergency services. A log records who enters."),
("At an accident scene, why should a guard note the position of anything he moved?",
 "So investigators know the scene changed", ["To claim it as lost property", "To bill the client", "It is never needed"],
 "Investigators reconstruct what happened from the scene. Knowing what was moved, and why, stops them drawing wrong conclusions."),
("A guard deals with a fight at 01:15 but is too busy to write anything until 03:00. What should his notebook show?",
 "The time he wrote the notes", ["Only 01:15, nothing else", "No time at all", "The time the shift began"],
 "Notes should show when they were made as well as when events happened, so a reader can judge how fresh the memory was. Pretending notes were written earlier is dishonest."),
("Which is the best way to write a time in an incident report?",
 "23:40", ["Late evening", "About midnight", "After the film"],
 "The 24-hour clock gives an exact, unambiguous time. Vague times weaken a report."),
("Which wording should a guard use in a report about a drunk patron?",
 "\"He swayed and slurred his words\"", ["\"He was totally wasted\"", "\"He is an alcoholic\"", "\"He was drunk as usual\""],
 "A report describes what the guard saw and heard. Labels and conclusions about drink or character are opinions that a court can challenge."),
("Why should a guard write his notebook in ink rather than pencil?",
 "Pencil can be rubbed out", ["Ink is quicker to write", "Pencil is not allowed on duty", "Ink looks neater to clients"],
 "Entries in ink cannot be erased and rewritten without it showing, so the notes stay credible if the Police or a court rely on them. A mistake is struck through once and initialled, never rubbed out."),
("Why are notebook pages numbered and kept in order?",
 "To show nothing was removed", ["To look neat and tidy", "To save on paper", "To count his shifts"],
 "Numbered, bound pages show the record is complete, which matters if the notes are used in court."),
("Which question best helps a guard get an accurate account from a witness?",
 "\"What did you see?\"", ["\"Was it the tall man?\"", "\"He ran left, didn't he?\"", "\"You saw him steal it, right?\""],
 "Open questions let the witness tell it in his own words. Leading questions suggest an answer and can change what he remembers."),
("Two witnesses describe a thief differently. What should the guard record?",
 "Both accounts as given", ["Only the one he prefers", "An average of the two", "Neither, as they conflict"],
 "The guard records each account in the witness's own words. Deciding what is true is for the police."),
("A guard hands a recovered wallet to a police officer. What should he record?",
 "Time, item and officer's name", ["Only that the case is closed", "The wallet owner's bank", "Nothing, police record it"],
 "Recording what was handed over, when and to whom keeps an unbroken record of who held the item, so its integrity can be shown in court."),
("A guard finds a dropped knife after a fight. What should he do if police are on the way?",
 "Leave it, guard it, note it", ["Wipe it clean and place it in a bag", "Put it in his pocket", "Throw it in a bin"],
 "Leaving the knife where it lies protects fingerprints and its position. If it must be made safe, he tells the police exactly what he did."),
("Besides the client, who may later rely on a guard's incident report?",
 "The police, insurers and courts", ["Only the guard's family", "The press, for a story", "Nobody once it is filed"],
 "A report may support a police investigation, an insurance claim or a court case, so it must be accurate, complete and factual."),
("Why should a report include what the guard did, not only what he saw?",
 "His acts are part of the record", ["It makes him look busy to the client", "It is never really needed", "It earns him overtime"],
 "Recording the guard's own actions, such as calling 112 or closing a door, explains the outcome and protects him."),
("A car speeds off after a theft. In what order should the guard note its details?",
 "Registration first", ["Colour of the seats first", "Number of passengers last", "The radio station first"],
 "The registration is lost from memory fastest and identifies the vehicle best, so it is noted first, then make, colour and direction."),
("After an incident a journalist asks the guard what happened. What should he do?",
 "Refer him to the client", ["Give him full details", "Show him the CCTV", "Name the suspect"],
 "Speaking to the media can harm an investigation and breach confidentiality. Media questions go to the client or employer."),
("Why should a guard keep witnesses apart before the police take statements?",
 "So accounts stay their own", ["To stop them leaving", "To save space in the lobby", "So they agree one story"],
 "Witnesses who talk together can change their memories to match. Separate accounts are more reliable evidence."),
]
